    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d569">

                <pb facs="0467" n="455" xml:id="pg455"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">740.00112 European War 1939/4927</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Assistant Chief of the Division of Near Eastern
                        Affairs</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Villard</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic"
                        >to the Chargé at Tangier</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Childs</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Washington</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1942-03-18">March 18, 1942</date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>
                    <hi rendition="#small-caps">Dear Rives</hi>: We have been greatly interested in
                    your telegram no. 116 of March 13, 1942, as well as all of your previous
                    telegrams on the subject thereof. The Department has been giving the most
                    careful consideration to the possibilities which you have outlined but at this
                    date it has not been possible to give you a definite answer.</p>
                <p>Just as soon as it is possible to do so, we shall communicate with you further.
                    In the meantime I wanted you to know that your telegrams have aroused the
                    greatest interest and are receiving all possible attention.</p>
                <p>Sincerely yours,</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>H. S. <hi rendition="#small-caps">Villard</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
